{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>I am Esav your firstborn.</b> One may say that for the need of the moment it is permissible to change one's words — for example, Abraham: 'Say of me, he is my brother'; and 'Let us take our journey, and let us return to you'; and similarly David, who confused his words with Achimelekh and said, 'The young men have been kept holy.' He also said he was going on a mission for the king. And Elisha too said to Hazael king of Aram, 'Go, say to him: you shall surely live' — yet HaShem showed him that he would kill. And similarly Micaiah, who said to Ahab, 'Go up and succeed' — rather, according to what they saw, so they spoke. Another interpretation: since the voice was the voice of Jacob, he did not deny him; thus he said to him <b>אנכי</b> in place of Esav your firstborn — for he has already sold me the birthright." "<b>In order that your soul may bless me.</b> But was Jacob serving on condition to receive a reward? Rather, Jacob said: I say to my father things that are between him and Esav, for he [Esav] said to him, 'In order that your soul may bless me.'" "<b>Tevarkheni.</b> The nun has a dagesh."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {0 "The phrase וכי יעקב משמש הימו על מנת לקבל פרס is grammatically difficult; translated hyper-literally as it stands.", 1 "The second-person pronoun נפשך in the lemma contrasts with נפשי in the biblical text; Chizkuni's text appears to reflect a variant reading of the lemma."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:06:20.052061557Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}